{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_5eb89ece2cacc3619e69c304358ea0b2114e1389df368c430dcbb067228689ac", "title": "Liebeslieder Walzer (ballet)", "text": "Liebeslieder Walzer is a ballet by New York City Ballet co-founder and ballet master George Balanchine, based on the Brahms' \"Liebeslieder Walzer\", Op. 52, and their sequel \"Neue Liebeslieder\", Op. 65.", "score": 54.5132}, {"id": "hp_doc_cd4e1c1aabbc51ef1a6add9d28a0115ef04aefceaab358a52dcf58397f21ec2d", "title": "Liebeslieder Walzer, Op. 52 (Brahms)", "text": "Johannes Brahms' Liebeslieder Waltzes (\"Liebeslieder-Walzer\") are distributed across two opus numbers: Op. 52 and Op. 65. The waltzes are a collection of love songs in Ländler style for voices and piano four hands. The lyrics for the \"Liebeslieder\" come from Georg Friedrich Daumer's \"Polydora\", a collection of folk songs and love poems. While there is no concrete record indicating the exact inspiration for the Waltzes, there is speculation that Brahms' motivation for the songs was his frustrated love for pianist Clara Schumann, composer Robert Schumann's wife.", "score": 50.1839}, {"id": "hp_doc_da4f0f6e01628f51c515adf16866b435205353689035b675a342c83a7bdabba4", "title": "Liebeslieder, Op. 114 (Strauss)", "text": "\"Liebeslieder \" op. 114 is a waltz by Johann Strauss II written in 1852 (not to be confused with Brahms's similarly titled \"Liebeslieder Walzer\" and \"Neue Liebeslieder\"). At the time it was conceived, the waltz was titled 'Liebesgedichte' or \"Love Poems\" and during its first performance, it was even announced as 'Liebesständchen' or \"Love Serenade\". The first performance was at the famed Vienna Volksgarten on 18 June 1852 under the composer's direction.", "score": 29.3446}], "remaining_searches": 2}
